<FieldNote title="What does this work need to satisfy?" description="The contract says what you agreed to deliver. Regulatory sources set requirements to consider. The scope sets out the work. Hyphos connects them." number="01" readTime="4 minute read" nextHref="/field-notes/what-should-ai-handle/" nextTitle="What should AI handle—and what should a person decide?">
  <div class="fn-callout"><span class="fn-label">The question behind the document</span><p>“What does this mean for the work we are doing—and what else depends on it?”</p></div>
  <h2>The answer crosses several sources.</h2>
  <p>A consulting engagement lead has a contract, a regulatory document and a draft scope open. A meeting added a client request; an AI conversation helped prepare the draft. Each contains useful information. The task is to understand how they fit together.</p>
  <p>What did we commit to? Which requirements apply? Does the scope cover them? Who owns the work, and what evidence will show it was completed?</p>
  <figure><div class="fn-flow"><div class="fn-step"><strong>The contract</strong><span>Interview the team and deliver a recommendations report.</span></div><div class="fn-step"><strong>The regulatory source</strong><span>Review personal information before sharing findings.</span></div><div class="fn-step"><strong>The draft scope</strong><span>Interviews, analysis and a report. No privacy review assigned.</span></div></div><figcaption>Illustrative consulting engagement. These contract terms, regulatory requirements and scope details are fictional.</figcaption></figure>
  <p>Read separately, these look like three ordinary documents. Connect them around the engagement, and there is a specific question to resolve: who reviews the interview material for personal information before the findings are shared?</p>
  <h2>Keep the obligation connected to the action.</h2>
  <p>Hyphos is designed to link a commitment or requirement to the scope item it affects, the work assigned to satisfy it and the supporting record. The original source remains part of that connection.</p>
  <ol><li><strong>Source.</strong> Which contract clause, regulatory provision or recorded agreement supports this? Keep the exact reference, version and relevant passage.</li><li><strong>Applicability.</strong> Why does it affect this project? Record the context and who confirmed it. A requirement for another location, activity or period may need a different treatment.</li><li><strong>Work.</strong> Which scope item and task address it? If no task or owner exists, make that gap visible.</li><li><strong>Evidence.</strong> What record supports completion or approval? Connect the report, review or decision to the obligation it addresses.</li></ol>
  <div class="fn-callout"><span class="fn-label">One connected thread</span><p>Source requirement → scope item → assigned work → review → supporting evidence.</p></div>
  <h2>A change should lead you to its consequences.</h2>
  <p>If the contract changes, the useful question is which scope items, tasks and deliverables need another look. If a regulatory source is updated, the team needs to review the work that relied on the earlier version.</p>
  <p>Those links give a reviewer a starting point. Conflicting sources or uncertain applicability remain questions to resolve, with the reasoning recorded for the next person.</p>
  <h2>The right version still matters.</h2>
  <p>A newer draft does not necessarily replace an approved scope. Microsoft’s document-management guidance treats version history and content approval as separate controls. The same distinction matters when linking work back to its basis: retain the version used for the decision as well as later changes. <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/sharepoint/libraries/plan-document-versioning-content-approval-and-check-out-controls-in-sharepoint">Read Microsoft’s explanation.</a></p>
  <h2>Try it with one deliverable.</h2>
  <p>Choose an advisory report, proposal or client service your team is preparing. Trace it back to what the contract promises and the requirements your team has identified as applicable. Then follow it forward to its owner, review and completion record.</p>
  <p>At each step, ask whether the connection is visible to the next person or exists only in someone’s memory. That is a useful place to start improving the workflow.</p>
  <h2>How this shapes Hyphos.</h2>
  <p>Whether the work is a consulting engagement, a client service or an internal initiative, contracts, regulations, conversations and documents become useful together when they remain connected to the work they affect. That is the relationship Hyphos is being built to carry, with Domas helping adapt the workflows and review steps to each business.</p>
  <p>We are developing with design partners. People bring the expertise to confirm requirements, resolve exceptions and approve consequential decisions; the system helps bring the relevant information and its relationships into view.</p>
  <details><summary>About this note and its source</summary><div><p>This is a practical Hyphos guide informed by our research into connected knowledge and document control. The scenario is fictional and makes no claim about a real regulation. The source–work–evidence sequence is our suggested way to examine a workflow.</p><p>The linked Microsoft guidance supports the distinction between versioning and approval. It is not evidence of Hyphos implementation or a certification checklist.</p></div></details>
</FieldNote>
